WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:09.400
CS702 Computational Interaction · Term project · Phase 2 prototype
Interactive Debugging and Steering of Multi-Agent AI Systems — re-implementation of AGDebugger (Epperson et al., CHI 2025). Team: Nguyen Nhat Minh, Do Duc Anh.

2
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:20.400
The problem: teams of LLM agents solve tasks by exchanging dozens of messages. One wrong step cascades, and a run of 50–90 messages ends with a wrong answer or no answer. Chat logs and print statements do not show where it went wrong, nor let the developer try a fix without starting over.

3
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:31.400
Target users: developers building and tuning multi-agent systems. Goal: find the message that derailed a run and steer the team to the correct result. Tasks: browse a long history and step the runtime; send messages to agents; reset the team to an earlier point, edit the offending message and continue; keep an overview of long runs and their branches.

4
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:53.400
Prototype: three components from the paper. C1 message history, sending and execution control. C2 reset-and-edit with session forking on real per-message checkpoints. C3 overview visualisation. Own implementation on AutoGen 0.7.5 + React; team under debug: Magentic-One orchestrator, Coder, Executor, WebSearcher; model Claude Haiku 5.5 at low effort.

5
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.000
Recorded failing run loaded (t13_fy_ratio_FAIL) — replayed from the saved history and checkpoints, no model calls.

6
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:12.000
C1 · Message history: every runtime message as a card — sender → recipient or topic, message type, payload. Tool calls and results included.

7
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Long payloads expand in place. Colour of the left bar = sending agent.

8
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:28.000
After 228 messages the run ends without the answer. Expected: 6651.7 (39,910 visitors in FY 2024/25 ÷ 6 instruments).

9
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.600
C1 · Sending: compose a broadcast (group topic) or a direct message to one agent; it lands in the runtime queue.

10
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.000
C1 · Execution control: Run processes the queue continuously, Step processes exactly one message, Drop discards the next one.

11
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.200
Dropped — the queue is empty again and the agents never saw the message.

12
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.400
C3 · Overview: one column per session, one rectangle per message in order. Legend buttons filter.

13
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.400
Colour by sender: orchestrator, Coder, Executor, WebSearcher, user.

14
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.400
Colour by recipient topic or agent.

15
00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.700
Colour by message type — tool calls stand out from text.

16
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:04.400
Hover shows the message; click scrolls the history to it.

17
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.400
Legend filter dims every other message type.

18
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:15.000
C2 · The mistake starts at t=9: the orchestrator's own plan assumes a published 'annual report or financial statements' with a financial-year total and tells the searcher not to estimate. The observatory only publishes calendar-year monthly tables — every later instruction inherits this wrong plan.

19
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.500
Edit the message in place: the fact sheet and plan are rewritten to point at the two monthly visitor reports, the financial-year definition and the facilities page.

20
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:28.300
Reset-and-edit: every agent's state is restored from the checkpoint taken before t=9, the edited message is re-published, and a new session (S1) forks off — the old branch stays viewable.

21
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.000
Step: the runtime processes just this one message (live model call).

22
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.800
Step processed the edited broadcast; the orchestrator's request-to-speak now waits in the queue. Run continues the branch to completion.

23
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:49.600
Steered branch finished. With the plan rewritten, the same team sums the monthly figures and reaches 6651.7 — the expected answer — instead of giving up after 228 messages. The paper's 'modify the plan' steering strategy.

24
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.200
Overview now shows both sessions; the amber marker is the fork point. Session tabs switch between branches.

25
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.500
The original failing branch is still browsable for comparison.

26
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:12.000
Status. Fully working: instrumented runtime with per-message checkpoints, C1, C2, recordings that replay without model calls. Basic: C3 overview. Harness: 29 tasks over a cached corpus and live Wikipedia; 97/97 Opus 5.5 runs succeeded, natural failures come from the Haiku 5.5 team. Next: more failing runs, E1 error-anchored context assembly, study instruments, pilot.
